; ======================================================================
;  hw_mem.pi4 - the Pi 4's answer to "how much memory is fitted".
;
;  The contract is the HwMemory* block in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi: one total in
;  BYTES, or -1 when the board honestly cannot know, and a fragment
;  saying where the number came from.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM, AND WHY NOT FROM THE OBVIOUS PLACE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE MAILBOX HAS A TAG THAT LOOKS LIKE THE ANSWER AND IS NOT. "Get ARM
;  memory" ($00010005) reports the ARM's SHARE OF THE FIRST GIGABYTE -
;  the split between the ARM and the VideoCore that gpu_mem in config.txt
;  sets. On a 4 GB board it answers about 948 MB, and a monitor that
;  printed that as the board's memory would be wrong by a factor of four
;  and would sound completely sure of itself. It is not asked here.
;
;  THE REVISION CODE IS THE ANSWER. The firmware's board revision word
;  carries a MEMSIZE field in bits 22:20, documented by Raspberry Pi
;  under "Raspberry Pi hardware - New-style revision codes" on
;  raspberrypi.com. The field is an exponent: the size is 256 MB shifted
;  left by it, so 0 is 256 MB, 1 is 512 MB, 2 is 1 GB, 3 is 2 GB, 4 is
;  4 GB and 5 is 8 GB. Consulted, not copied.
;
;  DECODED AS A SHIFT AND NOT AS A TABLE, for the same reason
;  PutRevision() in RaspberryPi4/Board/clock_info.pi4 already decodes the
;  same field as a shift and says so on the spot: "The memory field is a
;  power of two multiplying 256 MB, so it needs no table and cannot go
;  stale when a new size appears." A table would answer nothing for the
;  first board that ships a size nobody here has seen, and the two
;  decoders of one field in one monitor must not disagree.
;
;  AN OLD-STYLE CODE ANSWERS -1. Bit 23 says which scheme the word is in.
;  An old-style code is a small board number from a table this monitor
;  does not carry, and there is no memory field in it at all - so there
;  is nothing to decode and the honest answer is that this board cannot
;  say. PutRevision() refuses the same word for the same reason.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY IT IS ASKED ONCE AND REMEMBERED
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  A board does not grow memory between two commands, and the first
;  caller is the address guard (RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_addr.pi4), which
;  runs in front of every command that takes an address from the
;  operator. A mailbox round trip on each of those would put a firmware
;  transaction inside `memory`, `fill`, `copy`, `compare`, `crc32`,
;  `mtest` and the rest, and the day the firmware is slow to answer is
;  the day the monitor looks broken. So the first call asks and every
;  later one reads the remembered word.
;
;  IT ASKS QUIETLY, AND THAT IS WHY IT DOES NOT CALL MbxUp(). MbxUp()
;  (RaspberryPi4/Board/mailbox_info.pi4) PRINTS when the mailbox is down,
;  which is right for `info` and `clock` - a person typed those - and
;  wrong here, where the caller is a guard standing in front of somebody
;  else's command and two lines of mailbox prose would arrive with no
;  question in front of them. It is also included long after this file.
;  What the operator gets instead is HwMemoryWhere(), which says why
;  there is no number, in the one place that is asking.
;
;  MailboxInit() IS SAFE TO CALL HERE. It touches no peripheral at all -
;  it clears the library's own state, aligns the message buffer and reads
;  the tick frequency out of a system register - and it is asked for only
;  when MailboxReady() says the library has not been brought up. No
;  mailbox transaction can be in flight at that moment: every caller in
;  this monitor builds, sends and reads one message before returning.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4 (MailboxReady,
;  MailboxInit, MailboxBoardRevision), after Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi
;  (#HW_MEM_UNKNOWN) and after Anvil/Core/format.pbi (PrintDec), and
;  BEFORE RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_addr.pi4, which asks it. House rule 10:
;  this file includes nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT HAS BEEN ASKED, AND WHAT CAME BACK.
;
;  gPiMemAsked is 0 until the first question and 1 afterwards, whatever
;  the answer was - a board that could not say does not get asked again
;  every command. gPiMemBytes is meaningless until then and is
;  deliberately not initialised to a plausible size: an uninitialised
;  read must not look like an answer. *gPiMemWhy is the fragment
;  HwMemoryWhere() returns and is never left at 0 once asked.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global gPiMemAsked.i
Global gPiMemBytes.i
Global *gPiMemWhy

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMemFromRevision(rev) - the decode, and nothing else.
;
;  IT IS A PROCEDURE OF ITS OWN, and not four lines inside
;  HwMemoryBytes(), for the reason AddrForceArmedFor() in
;  Anvil/Core/addrsafe.pbi is: it is the only part of this that a gate
;  can EXECUTE. Everything around it talks to the firmware, and a
;  firmware is a board. This is pure - two shifts and a mask on one
;  word - so tools/hw_memory_emitted_check.py compiles this exact
;  procedure, runs the emitted A64 over every MEMSIZE code and over an
;  old-style word, and rejects a wrong shift, a wrong base and an
;  off-by-one code. A decode nothing can test is a decode nobody should
;  trust.
;
;  THE SHIFT IS OF A POSITIVE VALUE. rev is a firmware word below
;  $01000000 by the time the field is taken, so ">>" being arithmetic
;  cannot bite - the same note PutRevision() carries over the same
;  expression.
;
;  268435456 IS 256 MB WRITTEN OUT. It is the base of the exponent, in
;  bytes, and it is a literal here rather than a constant because a
;  constant in this compiler takes no arithmetic in its initialiser, so
;  "256 * 1024 * 1024" cannot be written where a reader would want it.
;  The largest answer this can produce is code 7, which is 32 GB, and
;  that is a long way inside a signed 64-bit .i.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMemFromRevision(rev.i)
  Define mem.i
  ; NEW-STYLE CODES ONLY. Bit 23 is the scheme bit; an old-style word has
  ; no memory field at all.
  If (rev & $00800000) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_MEM_UNKNOWN
  EndIf
  mem = (rev >> 20) & 7
  ProcedureReturn 268435456 << mem
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMemoryBytes() - the seam. Asked once; see the header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMemoryBytes()
  Define rev.i

  If gPiMemAsked <> 0
    ProcedureReturn gPiMemBytes
  EndIf
  gPiMemAsked = 1
  gPiMemBytes = #HW_MEM_UNKNOWN

  If MailboxReady() = 0
    If MailboxInit() = 0
      *gPiMemWhy = "the VideoCore mailbox did not come up"
      ProcedureReturn gPiMemBytes
    EndIf
  EndIf

  rev = MailboxBoardRevision()
  If rev = 0
    *gPiMemWhy = "the firmware would not say what board this is"
    ProcedureReturn gPiMemBytes
  EndIf

  gPiMemBytes = HwMemFromRevision(rev)
  If gPiMemBytes = #HW_MEM_UNKNOWN
    *gPiMemWhy = "an old-style revision code carries no memory field"
    ProcedureReturn gPiMemBytes
  EndIf

  *gPiMemWhy = "the board revision code the firmware reports"
  ProcedureReturn gPiMemBytes
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMemoryWhere() - where that number came from, or why there is none.
;
;  IT ASKS HwMemoryBytes() FIRST rather than trusting the caller to have
;  done so. A provenance fragment read before the question was asked
;  would be 0, and a caller printing it would print nothing at all -
;  which is the one outcome this seam exists to prevent. Asking is free
;  after the first time.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMemoryWhere()
  HwMemoryBytes()
  ProcedureReturn *gPiMemWhy
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieTellMemory() - hand the size to the PCI Express driver.
;
;  WHY THE DRIVER IS TOLD AND DOES NOT ASK. RaspberryPi4/Lib/pcie.pi4
;  sizes its inbound DMA window from the memory fitted, exactly as Linux
;  and U-Boot do; it is a library, the size is a board fact, and the
;  include order settles it anyway - pcie.pi4 is compiled hundreds of
;  lines before hal.pi4 and this file. So the board hands the number
;  over, and every diagnostic that includes the library on its own is
;  left with the driver's own fallback and behaves exactly as before.
;
;  IT LIVES HERE AND NOT AT THE CALL SITES because there are two of them
;  - UsbEnumerate() in RaspberryPi4/Board/cursor_input.pi4 and the
;  fallback walk in RaspberryPi4/Board/storage.pi4 - and the sentence
;  below has to be the same sentence whichever one got there first. This
;  file is where this board's memory facts are published to the parts of
;  the machine that need them.
;
;  CALL IT BEFORE PcieInit(). After the window is programmed the number
;  can only make the check and the window disagree, which is the exact
;  shape of forum 916.
;
;  THE NOTE IS PRINTED ONCE PER BOOT, and only when the size is unknown.
;  Twice would be once per call site, which tells the reader something
;  about this monitor's structure and nothing about his board; and on a
;  board that knows its size there is nothing to say here at all -
;  `info` prints the size and the window follows it silently.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global gPiMemPcieNoted.i

Procedure PcieTellMemory()
  PcieSetMemoryBytes(HwMemoryBytes())
  If HwMemoryBytes() <> #HW_MEM_UNKNOWN
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gPiMemPcieNoted <> 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  gPiMemPcieNoted = 1
  PrintN("!! this board could not say how much memory it has, so the PCI Express")
  PrintN("   inbound window is programmed at its largest useful size instead of")
  PrintN("   being fitted to the memory. That is the safe direction - a window")
  PrintN("   bigger than the memory behind it costs nothing - and transfers are")
  PrintN("   still refused above the 3 GB the wrapper erratum allows. Type info")
  PrintN("   to see why the size is unknown.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PutMemBytes(b) - a byte count as a person would say it.
;
;  "4 GB", "512 MB". Not 4294967296, and not 4096 MB: the number on the
;  box is the number to print, and every size this field can carry is an
;  exact power of two multiplying 256 MB, so there is never a remainder
;  to round away or to hide.
;
;  IT PRINTS RATHER THAN RETURNING A FRAGMENT because the value is
;  computed and a returned fragment would have to be assembled into a
;  buffer that somebody then has to own. Two callers print it: the RAM
;  row of `info` and nothing else today.
;
;  1048576 IS ONE MEGABYTE. The divide is exact for every size this
;  decoder can produce; it is done in megabytes rather than in bytes so
;  that a 32 GB board's byte count cannot meet a divisor large enough to
;  matter.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PutMemBytes(b.i)
  Define mb.i
  If b < 0
    Print("an unknown amount")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  mb = b / 1048576
  If mb >= 1024
    PrintDec(mb / 1024)
    Print(" GB")
  Else
    PrintDec(mb)
    Print(" MB")
  EndIf
EndProcedure
